      "text_raw": "Tuning Q is much more\ncomplicated and therefore\nnot used very often.\nAs explained before it can\nbe achieved by tuning a\nresistor which is in parallel\nwith the filter load capaci-\ntance C .\nL\nThe problem is however,\nhow to measure the Q. For\nthis purpose, an under-\ndamped second-order sys-\ntem is realized by means\nof two Gm blocks. For low\nQ its response is oscillatory.\nBoth the expression and the\nresponse versus time are given in this slide.\nThe oscillatory behavior can be detected by taking the difference between the envelop and the\naverage output signal. The measurement has to be carried out with two different time constants,\none which provides the average, and one which takes the envelop.",
      "text": "Tuning Q is much more complicated and therefore not used very often. As explained before it can be achieved by tuning a resistor which is in parallel with the filter load capacitance C . L The problem is however, how to measure the Q. For this purpose, an underdamped second-order system is realized by means of two Gm blocks. For low Q its response is oscillatory. Both the expression and the response versus time are given in this slide. The oscillatory behavior can be detected by taking the difference between the envelop and the average output signal. The measurement has to be carried out with two different time constants, one which provides the average, and one which takes the envelop."
    }
  ],
